INT. SLEEPER CAR - ORIENT NIGHT EXPRESS - 2 A.M.

Gaslight steady. Curtains drawn. The train hammers through a tunnel.

PRUDENCE ACHTERBERG, 50s, in a quilted robe, grips a teacup. Across from her, COLONEL ISAAC DUBOIS-KANE, 70, in pajamas and monocle, thumbs a revolver. LALITA SENGUPTA, 20s, a porter's daughter in borrowed uniform, stands guard by the door. ENZO MARCHETTI, 30s, a violinist, sits sweating by the window. CECILE VANTERPOOL, 40s, in evening gown, lies on the floor between them. Not moving.

PRUDENCE
Colonel, put that down. You'll shoot the wrong one.

DUBOIS-KANE
There is no wrong one, madam. Only the guilty and the uncooperative.

ENZO
It was her wine, I tell you. She drank first.

LALITA
She did not drink first. You poured.

ENZO
I poured for everyone!

LALITA
You poured for her twice.

PRUDENCE
Lalita, dear, you are the only one not drinking.

LALITA
I'm on shift, Madam Achterberg.

DUBOIS-KANE
Then why do you stand at the door?

LALITA
Because the last one to leave a murder is the first one accused.

A long beat. The train bucks. The cup rattles in its saucer. Cecile's fingers twitch.

PRUDENCE
Colonel. Her hand.

DUBOIS-KANE
(pocketing the revolver)
By Jove. She's alive.

CECILE
(from the floor, faint)
Of course I'm alive. Someone get me out of this dress.
